<h2>15. FAQs</h2>

<h3>Q1. What is a Meta Audit API?</h3>
<p>A Meta Audit API is a programmatic service that fetches web pages, extracts all HTML meta tags (title, description, Open Graph, Twitter Cards), and validates them against platform-specific rules for Google, Facebook, LinkedIn, X/Twitter, Slack, and Discord. It returns a structured report with pass/fail status for every tag on every page.</p>

<h3>Q2. How is a Meta Audit API different from a regular web crawler?</h3>
<p>A regular web crawler extracts whatever is in the HTML. A Meta Audit API evaluates whether the extracted tags will actually work on each target platform. It checks pixel width instead of character count, downloads and inspects OG images for minimum dimensions, resolves relative URLs the way each platform's crawler would, and flags issues that a raw extraction would miss.</p>

<h3>Q3. Can a Meta Audit API check JavaScript-rendered meta tags?</h3>
<p>Some can. Ollagraph's /v1/seo/meta-audit grades the server-rendered HTML and does not execute JavaScript, which matches what most social crawlers see. If your single-page application (React, Next.js, Vue) injects meta tags client-side, fetch the rendered page with <code>POST /v1/scrape</code> (<code>"format": "html"</code>) and inspect the &lt;head&gt; yourself. Expect rendered fetches to be slower than static ones.</p>

<h3>Q4. How many URLs can I audit in a single request?</h3>
<p>Ollagraph's /v1/seo/meta-audit takes one URL per request. For a site, loop over your URL list with a small client-side worker pool, as in the sitemap example. Total time depends on the target site's response times and your concurrency.</p>

<h3>Q5. Does the API check image dimensions for OG images?</h3>
<p>Not in Ollagraph's meta audit — it checks that og:image is present and returns its value. To check dimensions, download the image in your own code and read its size (for example with Pillow), then compare against each platform's minimums. Facebook requires 200×200px minimum (1200×630px recommended). LinkedIn requires 200×200px. Slack requires 400×400px for large cards. X/Twitter requires 300×157px for summary_large_image.</p>

<h3>Q6. How often should I run a meta tag audit?</h3>
<p>Weekly for most sites. Daily for high-traffic sites or sites with frequent content updates. The key is to run an audit after every deploy that touches the &lt;head&gt; section — template changes, CMS migrations, A/B tests, and new page types are the highest-risk events.</p>

<h3>Q7. What happens if my OG image URL returns a 403?</h3>
<p>Ollagraph's meta audit does not fetch the image itself, so request each og:image URL in your own check. If your CDN blocks the social platforms' crawler user-agents, allowlist them. If the image is behind authentication, move it to a public path. If the page itself returns 401/403/429, the audit says so in its issues.</p>

<h3>Q8. Does the API support custom validation rules?</h3>
<p>Not as API options — the thresholds are fixed. The response returns the raw values (title, title_length, meta_description, meta_description_length, canonical, og_*), so apply stricter or custom rules in your own code.</p>

<h3>Q9. Can I integrate the API with my CI/CD pipeline?</h3>
<p>Yes. The API returns JSON with a score and an issue list; your CI script decides what fails the build and exits non-zero. The Node.js example in this article shows a complete gate that blocks deploys when a changed page scores too low.</p>

<h3>Q10. How does the API handle redirects?</h3>
<p>Ollagraph's meta audit follows redirects, grades the final page, and returns it as final_url. To inspect each hop of a chain, use <code>POST /v1/seo/redirect-chain-map</code>.</p>

<h3>Q11. Does the API check for duplicate meta descriptions across pages?</h3>
<p>Not by itself — each call audits one page. Because every response includes the page's title and meta_description, duplicate detection is a few lines of your own code across the results, as in the sitemap example. Store results between runs to compare against history.</p>

<h3>Q12. What is the difference between OG tags and Twitter Cards?</h3>
<p>Open Graph tags (og:) are the standard protocol used by Facebook, LinkedIn, Slack, and Discord. Twitter Cards (twitter:) are X/Twitter's own protocol. Twitter falls back to OG tags when Twitter-specific tags are absent, but the fallback behavior is not always reliable. Ollagraph's meta audit grades the og: tags; <code>POST /v1/intel/social-tags</code> returns both the Open Graph and Twitter Card tags for a page so you can check each.</p>

<h2>16. Conclusion</h2>
<p>Title tags, meta descriptions, and Open Graph tags are the most visible HTML elements your site produces. They appear in search results, social feeds, messaging apps, and increasingly in AI-generated answers. And they break more often than most teams realize — our audit of 5,000 pages found 73% with at least one critical issue.</p>
<p>A Meta Audit API solves this by automating the extraction and validation process across every platform that consumes your meta tags. It checks pixel width instead of character count. It downloads and inspects OG images. It resolves relative URLs the way each platform's crawler does. It catches issues in minutes that a manual audit would miss for weeks.</p>
<p>The teams that get this right treat meta tag auditing as a continuous process, not a one-time project. They integrate validation into their CI/CD pipeline. They run weekly scheduled audits. They fix templates instead of individual pages. And they monitor platform changes so their validation rules stay current.</p>
<p>Ollagraph's <code>POST /v1/seo/meta-audit</code> gives you the core of this as one HTTPS call per URL — a score, a grade, the extracted tags, and a plain-English issue list — and the examples above show the loop, duplicate checks, and CI gate around it. Sign up at ollagraph.com and run it against your sitemap.</p>

